"perf(mod_arith): Goldilocks add dominates hash kernels — can NVPTX use the carry flag? · Issue #409 · fractalyze/prime-ir · GitHub (https://github.com/fractalyze/prime-ir/issues/409)\nciteturn11view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"lineno\":250}); Total lines: 371\nL117: ## Repository navigation\nL118: \nL119:   *   * 【62†Code】\nL120:   * 【63†Issues31 (31)】\nL121:   * 【64†Pull requests5 (5)】\nL122:   * 【65†Discussions】\nL123:   * 【66†Actions】\nL124:   * 【67†Projects】\nL125:   * 【68†Security and quality】\nL126:   * 【69†Insights】\nL127: \nL128: More items\nL129: \nL130: # perf(mod_arith): Goldilocks add dominates hash kernels — can NVPTX use the carry flag? #409\nL131: \nL132: New issue\nL133: \nL134: Copy link\nL135: \nL136: New issue\nL137: \nL147: ## Description\nL148: \nL149: 【71†erkhesbold2005-blip】\nL150: \nL151: opened 【72†on Jul 29, 2026】\nL152: \nL153: Issue body actions\nL154: \nL155: ### Pre-submission checklist\nL156: \nL157:   * [Input] I have searched the existing issues and this is not a duplicate.\nL158: ### Context / problem\nL159: \nL160: A Goldilocks addition chain reduces after every term, which makes modular\nL161: addition — not multiplication — the dominant cost in hash-heavy kernels.\nL162: \nL163: Measured on the Poseidon2 width-16 permutation (the kernel behind every merkle\nL164: tree in zisk-zorch's prover, RTX 5090, dumped PTX from the `frx` GPU plugin):\nL165: \nL166:     `831  setp        4792 instructions total\nL167:     828  selp\nL168:     148  mul.hi.u64\nL169:     `\nL170: ~3,300 of 4,792 instructions are the add-and-carry-fold pattern, against 148\nL171: multiplies. The emitted idiom per addition is:\nL172: \nL173:     `add.s64     %rd56, %rd55, %rd17        // a + b, wraps mod 2^64\nL174:     setp.lt.u64 %p7,   %rd56, %rd55        // did it wrap?\nL175:     selp.b64    %rd57, 4294967295, 0, %p7  // if so we owe +ε (ε = 2^32−1)\nL176:     add.s64     %rd58, %rd57, %rd56        // fold ε back in\nL177:     `\nL178: This is already the good path — `ConvertAdd`'s `wantLazyGl64` branch, which\nL179: avoids the canonicalizing subtract. The problem is not that it is unreduced, it\nL180: is that the fold happens per term and serially: an MDS row summing k terms\nL181: emits k carry-folds in a dependency chain (`rd56→rd58→rd59→rd61→rd64…`), so the\nL182: cost is 4 instructions and one serialized latency per term.\nL183: Consequence for the prover: Poseidon2 sustains ~0.97 G perm/s, which is ~5–6% of\nL184: the device's integer-multiply peak, and hashing alone is 39.2 ms of a 69 ms\nL185: proof (【73†fractalyze/zisk-zorch#108】).\nL186: ### Proposed solution\nL187: \nL188: Accumulate a Goldilocks addition chain in 128 bits and reduce once at the\nL189: end, instead of folding the carry after each term.\nL190: \nL191: For a chain `t0 + t1 + … + tk`:\nL192: \nL193:   * carry the accumulator as a `(hi, lo)` pair, each term costing `add.cc.u64` +\nL194: `addc.u64` — 2 instructions, and the carry-out is consumed by the hardware\nL195: flag rather than a `setp`/`selp` pair;\nL196:   * perform one Goldilocks 128→64 reduction at the end of the chain, which the\nL197: Solinas structure (p = 2^64 − 2^32 + 1) makes cheap.\nL198: A 16-term MDS row goes from ~64 instructions to ~37, and the per-term serial\nL199: dependency shortens to a two-instruction carry propagate.\nL200: \nL201: This is a `BoundMap` extension: today lazy state is tracked within the 64-bit\nL202: carrier (`kp`/`umax`), and `wantLazyGl64` explicitly requires that at most one\nL203: operand be non-canonical to avoid double-overflow. A 128-bit accumulator state\nL204: lifts that restriction for the chain interior and only materializes a canonical\nL205: value where a consumer demands one.\nL206: ### Alternatives considered\nL207:   * Cheaper carry fold within 64 bits. `add.cc.u64` + `addc.u64` +\nL208: `mad.lo.u64 c, ε, sum` is 3 instructions instead of 4, and keeps the current\nL209: BoundMap contract. Strictly smaller change, but it leaves the serial\nL210: dependency per term and captures maybe a third of the win. Worth taking if\nL211: the 128-bit accumulator proves too invasive.\nL212:   * Do nothing in prime-ir; restructure Poseidon2 in the emitter. The\nL213: permutation's ILP and register pressure are a separate, real problem\nL214: (occupancy limits how many permutations stay in flight). But it is orthogonal\nL215: — the instruction count per permutation is set here, and both are needed to\nL216: reach the throughput target.\nL217:   * Accept the cost and buy throughput with occupancy alone. Does not address\nL218: that we issue ~3× more instructions than the arithmetic requires.\nL219: ### Why this approach\nL220: \nL221: It targets the measured majority of the instruction stream with a\nL222: transformation that is local to one lowering pattern and provably\nL223: value-preserving (the chain's mathematical result is unchanged; only where the\nL224: reduction happens moves). Every consumer of Goldilocks addition chains benefits\nL225: — Poseidon2, the MDS layers, and the field-arithmetic-heavy constraint kernels\nL226: — without touching any of them.\nL227: ### Plan\nL228:   1. Extend `BoundMap` with a 128-bit accumulator state (hi/lo) alongside the\nL229: existing `kp`/`umax` tracking.\nL230:   2. In `ConvertAdd`, when the result feeds another add and the consumer tolerates\nL231: non-canonical input, emit `add.cc`/`addc` into the accumulator instead of the\nL232: carry-fold.\nL233:   3. Materialize with one 128→64 Solinas reduction at the chain boundary — where\nL234: a multiply, comparison, or store consumes the value.\nL235:   4. Gate on the existing byte-match suites: Poseidon2 goldens and the\nL236: zisk-zorch commit-root byte-match (a stage-1 root is a Poseidon2 image of\nL237: 8.4 M rows, so any divergence shows immediately).\nL238:   5. Re-measure the permutation; expected ~1.5× on this kernel.\nL239: ### Acceptance criteria\nL240: \nL241:   * Goldilocks addition chains emit one reduction per chain rather than per term.\nL242:   * Poseidon2 w16 throughput improves measurably from the 0.97 G perm/s baseline;\nL243: target ≥1.4 G perm/s.\nL244:   * All existing byte-match goldens unchanged — this must be bit-identical.\nL245:   * No regression for non-Goldilocks moduli (the path is Solinas-gated).\nL246: ### References\nL247: \nL248:   * 【73†perf: close the wall-clock gap to native pil2 — overlap work across instances zisk-zorch#108】 — the wall-clock gap this feeds; hashing is 39.2 ms\nL249: of a 69 ms proof and is at parity with pil2's own CUDA, so the remaining\nL250: lever is throughput per permutation.\nL251:   * `ModArithToArith.cpp::ConvertAdd`, the `wantLazyGl64` branch — where the\nL252: per-term carry fold is emitted today.\nL253: \nL254: ### Area\nL255: \nL256: compiler\nL257: \nL258: ### Parent issue\nL259: \nL260: No response\nL261: \nL262: ### Milestone\nL263: \nL264: No response\nL265: ### Relevant knowledge\nL266: \nL267: No response\nL268: \nL269: Reactions are currently unavailable\nL270: ## Activity\nL271: \nL272:   1. ### erkhesbold2005-blip commented on Jul 29, 2026\nL273: \nL274: 【71†erkhesbold2005-blip】\nL275: \nL276: 【74†on Jul 29, 2026】\nL277: \nL278: Contributor Author\nL279: \nL280: More actions\nL281: \nL282: Correcting this before anyone implements it — the stated win is unproven, and my estimate in the description was speculation.\nL283: \nL284: I A/B'd the two accumulation strategies directly (RTX 5090, values byte-identical in both runs):\nL285: test  | per-term reduce  | 128-bit accumulate  | ratio\nL286: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL287: 16-term chain, one array per term, N=2^22  | 0.39 ms  | 0.38 ms  | 1.02×\nL288: 256-add dependent chain, register-resident, N=2^20  | 0.09 ms  | 0.20 ms  | 0.46×\nL289: \nL290: The first test was worthless: 16 arrays × 2^22 × 8 B = 537 MB, so both sides sat on the bandwidth roofline (~0.36 ms) and the arithmetic was invisible. That is the regime to avoid when measuring this.\nL291: The second is the regime a permutation actually runs in — state in registers across rounds — and there the 128-bit form is 2.2× slower.\nL292: Why, and why it does not refute the underlying idea: a Python/frx emulation must detect the carry in software (`s < lo`, convert, accumulate into `hi`), which costs the same ~4 instructions as today's `+ε` fold plus an extra live register in an already register-starved kernel. The proposal's actual saving depends on the hardware carry flag — `add.cc.u64` / `addc.u64` chaining without a compare — which cannot be expressed from the frontend at all. Only the lowering can emit it.\nL293: So what remains true, and what does not:\nL294:      * Still true (measured): the w16 permutation emits 831 `setp` + 828 `selp` against 148 `mul.hi.u64` out of 4792 instructions, and sustains ~0.97 G perm/s ≈ 5–6% of integer-multiply peak. Modular addition genuinely dominates the instruction stream.\nL295:      * Not established: that restructuring the accumulation saves instructions. Today's `AddUIExtendedOp` lowers to `add` + `setp.lt` (comparison-based overflow) rather than `add.cc`/`addc`. That is the thing worth checking first — whether NVPTX can be made to use the carry flag for `arith.addui_extended` — because it is a much smaller change and it is the only part with a plausible instruction saving.\nL296: Suggest re-scoping this issue to that question, and not to the 128-bit accumulator, until a compiler-side experiment shows a win. Reproducers: `add_chain_ab.py` (bandwidth-bound, do not trust) and `add_chain_reg.py` (register-resident) — happy to attach.\nL297: \nL298:   2. 【71†erkhesbold2005-blip】\nL299: \nL300: changed the title ~~[-]perf(mod_arith): accumulate Goldilocks add chains in 128 bits, reduce once[/-]~~ [+]perf(mod_arith): Goldilocks add dominates hash kernels — can NVPTX use the carry flag?[/+] 【75†on Jul 29, 2026】\nL301:   3. ### erkhesbold2005-blip commented on Jul 30, 2026\nL302: \nL303: 【71†erkhesbold2005-blip】\nL304: \nL305: 【76†on Jul 30, 2026】\nL306: \nL307: Contributor Author\nL308: \nL309: More actions\nL310: \nL311: Measured the carry-flag hypothesis directly. It is also refuted — the current lowering is the better one.\nL312: \nL313: Two facts first, both confirmed:\nL314:      * NVPTX does have 64-bit extended-precision arithmetic — it emits `add.cc.s64` / `addc.cc.s64` for an `i128` add — and `ptxas` accepts hand-written `add.cc.u64`/`addc.u64` on sm_120a. The capability is there.\nL315:      * For `llvm.uadd.with.overflow.i64` it nevertheless emits `add.s64` + `setp.lt.u64`, i.e. exactly what we see in the Poseidon2 PTX. So the lowering choice is real, not an accident of our IR.\nL316: Then I benchmarked the two forms as raw PTX kernels — identical arithmetic, 512 dependent rounds × 4 independent lanes per thread, 2^20 threads, sm_120a, `-O3`, outputs byte-identical:\nL317: \nL318: form  | instrs/add  | time\nL319: --- | --- | ---\nL320: today: `add` / `setp.lt` / `selp` / `add`  | 4  | 0.318 ms\nL321: carry: `add.cc.u64` / `addc.u64` / `mad.lo.u64`  | 3  | 0.443 ms\nL322: The 3-instruction carry form is 1.4× slower. The reason is that the carry flag is a single architectural resource: `add.cc`/`addc` serialize against each other, so the four independent lanes cannot interleave. The compare-and-select form uses ordinary registers and predicates, which the scheduler overlaps freely. Fewer instructions, less parallelism, net loss.\nL323: One more number worth recording, because it reframes the original problem: in that benchmark the current form sustains ~6.8 G adds/s = ~27 T instructions/s, roughly half of this device's integer-op peak. The Goldilocks add lowering is not inefficient — in isolation it runs well.\nL324: That means the permutation's ~5–6% overall ALU utilization is not attributable to how additions are lowered. Both hypotheses in this issue are now disproven by measurement, and the remaining explanation is the permutation's own structure — dependency chains through the rounds and register pressure limiting how many permutations stay in flight.\nL325: Closing this as measured-and-rejected. The live question moves to the Poseidon2 emitter's occupancy and ILP, which is a different component; recording the numbers here so nobody re-derives them. Reproducers: `gen_carry_bench.py` + `run_carry_bench.py`.\nL326: \nL327:   4. 【71†erkhesbold2005-blip】\nL328: \nL329: closed this as 【77†not planned】【78†on Jul 30, 2026】\nL330: \nL331: to join this conversation on GitHub. Already have an account? 【79†Sign in to comment】\nL332: ## Metadata\nL333: \nL334: ## Metadata\nL335: \nL336: ### Assignees\nL337: \nL338: No one assigned\nL339: \nL340: ### Labels\nL341: \nL342: No labels\nL343: \nL344: No labels\nL345: \nL346: ### Type\nL347: \nL348: No type\nL349: \nL350: ### Projects\nL351: \nL352: No projects\nL353: \nL354: ### Milestone\nL355: \nL356: No milestone\nL357: \nL358: ### Relationships\nL359: \nL360: None yet\nL361: \nL362: ### Development\nL363: \nL364: No branches or pull requests\nL365: \nL366: ## Issue actions\nL367: \nL368:   * 【80†Image†github.githubassets.com】Open in GitHub Copilot app\nL369: \nL370: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[libcu++] Fix `arch_traits` for sm100 (#11480) (#11484) · NVIDIA/cccl@28101b0 · GitHub (https://github.com/NVIDIA/cccl/commit/28101b07413743e1dcca8ca8af1cfc63e8cff2d1)\nciteturn11view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn9view2\",\"id\":62}); Total lines: 225\n"
